# REGRESSION FIXTURE — the BASE-SHAPE axis of an `&<projection>` call argument.
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12). Family-1's discriminator is the projected TYPE, not the
# projection FORM — Scout A measured every genuine projection form giving the
# SAME per-type verdict. This fixture is the form axis's own net: it holds the
# type fixed at the broken side (`int`, a by-value field) and varies the ROOT /
# BASE shape instead, so a future change that fixes one base shape and not
# another cannot pass. Individually low-yield, deliberately; the point is that
# the axis is NAMED and enumerated rather than silently sampled.
#
# Cells covered here:
#   1. nested struct field        `&o.inner.fd`
#   2. `&self.fd` inside a method
#   3. `&`-param base             `void f(Holder &h): inc(&h.fd)`
#   4. `!`-param base             `void f(Holder !h): inc(&h.fd)`
#   5. module-level `static` base `&G.fd`
#   6. struct field under a collection element `&v[0].fd`
#   7. TUPLE-ROOTED projection    `&t.0[0]`  ← added late; see its inline note.
#      It was NOT covered when this fixture first claimed the base-shape axis,
#      and it was genuinely broken (10, want 11) at base and at the first
#      Family-1 tip. A fixture that names an axis must sample it.
#      ⚠ A SECOND UNCOVERED PRODUCT CELL, and this one is MEMORY-UNSAFE: row 8's
#      Guard object is sampled only at a guard LOCAL root. The same object form
#      at a `&`-PARAM root (`void f(Guard[Inner] &g): inc(&g.fd)`) SIGSEGVs on C
#      and hard-fails `llc` on LLVM, `gg check` clean, at base and after this
#      round alike. Filed with a committed repro
#      (`known_gaps/sound_guard_param_field_unsafe.gg`); named here because this
#      fixture enumerates the root-shape axis and must not imply that cell is
#      covered by row 8.
#      ⚠ THE WRAPPER-KIND AXIS IS SAMPLED AT ONE VALUE OF THREE.
#      `guard_inner_suffix` matches `Guard__`, `ReadGuard__` and `WriteGuard__`;
#      row 8 exercises `Guard` only.
#        · `WriteGuard[T]` — write-capable, same branch, expected to behave as
#          row 8 does. NOT sampled here: obtaining one needs an `RWLock[T]`
#          receiver, which did not resolve in this fixture's context, and
#          chasing that is a separate thread rather than a base-shape row. Cell
#          named per Core #12 rather than left implied.
#        · `ReadGuard[T]` — deliberately NOT sampled: writes through it are
#          silently DROPPED rather than rejected (both faces, `gg check` clean,
#          base and tip identical), which is a filed Core #10 lower-or-reject
#          defect. Asserting today's behaviour would wire the defect in as
#          canonical; the reference-grade fix is a check-time rejection.
#   8. GUARD AUTO-DEREF object     `&g.fd`   ← added later still, same lesson,
#      and the sharpest instance of it: broken at base and through THREE fix
#      rounds while `g.fd = v` worked. It is the only object form resolved by a
#      TYPE-level branch instead of a syntactic `Expr::` arm, so neither arm-set
#      lint can see it — an omission that three shipped comments called harmless.
#
# Cells covered ELSEWHERE, named here so the omission is explicit rather than
# silent (Core #12 wants each omission justified, not invisible):
#   * BARE-param base — `known_gaps/sound_amp_bareparam_root_materialize.gg`.
#     It is deliberately NOT here because it must MATERIALIZE (11 / 10), not
#     write through, and mixing it into a write-through fixture is how a fix that
#     writes through in both directions passes everything.
#     ⚠ THE PRODUCT CELL IS UNCOVERED AND KNOWN-BROKEN: a BARE `Box[T]` param
#     crossed with a `(*box).fd` projection (`void f(Box[Holder] b):
#     inc(&(*b).fd)`) prints `10 / 10` at base AND after this round — the write
#     is lost INSIDE the callee too, so it is not even the materialize
#     behaviour, just a dropped write. Same shape as the materialize trap's
#     defect, one costume over. Named here rather than added because asserting
#     it would require deciding and implementing the materialize semantics for a
#     `Box` root, which is its own track; this fixture's rows all write through
#     and it must not silently imply that cell does too.
#   * nested collection element `&vv[0][0]` — `cow_amp_projection_type_axis.gg`.
#   * `&(*box).fd` — `cow_amp_deref_box_projection.gg`.
#   * `&t.0.fd` (struct field under a TUPLE) and `&v[0].0` (tuple field under a
#     collection element) — NOT covered by any live fixture and NOT fixed by
#     Family-1: they are the Family-2 resolver-arm-matrix defect (the two place
#     resolvers are a mutually-recursive pair differing by exactly two arms).
#     Both have committed `#[ignore]`d repros; filed in TODO.md.
#   * `&v.get(0).unwrap().fd` (get-chain) — also unfixed, filed, its own repro.
#
# RED-VERIFIED against the pre-fix compiler, which printed
#     10 / 10 / 10 / 10 / 10 / 10 / 10 / 10
# — every base shape lost the write. Post-fix all eight print 11. ⚠ Rows 7 and 8
# each needed a FURTHER fix beyond the original chokepoint and were still
# printing 10 after it, so they are RED-verified against LATER tips as well as
# the pre-round compiler: row 7 needed the `TupleFieldAccess` arm in
# `place_expr_type_only`, and row 8 needed that function's GUARD auto-deref
# branch — which no arm-set lint could have demanded, because the guard
# projection is resolved by a TYPE-level branch rather than an `Expr::` arm.
#
# Seeds are computed, never literals.
struct Inner:
    int fd

struct Holder:
    int fd
    Inner inner

static Holder G = Holder(10, Inner(10))

int seed():
    return 10

void inc(int &n):
    n = n + 1

equip Holder:
    void bump_self(&self):
        inc(&self.fd)

void via_amp(Holder &h):
    inc(&h.fd)

void via_move(Holder !h):
    inc(&h.fd)
    print(h.fd)

void main():
    # 1. nested struct field
    Holder o = Holder(seed(), Inner(seed()))
    inc(&o.inner.fd)
    print(o.inner.fd)

    # 2. &self.fd inside a method
    Holder m = Holder(seed(), Inner(seed()))
    m.bump_self()
    print(m.fd)

    # 3. &-param base
    Holder a = Holder(seed(), Inner(seed()))
    via_amp(&a)
    print(a.fd)

    # 4. !-param base (the callee observes its own write)
    Holder b = Holder(seed(), Inner(seed()))
    via_move(!b)

    # 5. module-level static base
    inc(&G.fd)
    print(G.fd)

    # 6. struct field under a collection element
    Vector[Inner] v = Vector[Inner]()
    v.push(Inner(seed()))
    inc(&v[0].fd)
    print(v[0].fd)

    # 7. TUPLE-ROOTED projection — `&t.0[0]`, a collection element under a
    #    tuple field. Added because this fixture CLAIMS the base-shape axis and
    #    a reader would otherwise take tuple roots as covered when they were not
    #    (Core #12: a fixture's NAME is a claim about SCOPE). It was measurably
    #    NOT covered: this row printed 10 at base AND at the first Family-1 tip,
    #    because `place_expr_type_only` had no `TupleFieldAccess` arm, so the
    #    index resolver's type-only pre-check declined and the whole projection
    #    fell through to a value copy. The same one arm that fixed the
    #    auto-propagate tuple costume fixes this.
    (Vector[int], int) tup = (Vector[int](), 1)
    tup.0.push(seed())
    inc(&tup.0[0])
    print(tup.0[0])

    # 8. GUARD AUTO-DEREF object — `&g.fd`, where the field lives on the GUARDED
    #    value and NOT on the `Guard[T]` wrapper. This cell was broken at base
    #    AND through three fix rounds of this round (printed the seed, write
    #    lost) while the ASSIGN face `g.fd = v` worked — Family-1's own
    #    served-at-one-face signature, surviving in the one object form whose
    #    resolution is TYPE-driven rather than a syntactic `Expr::` arm, which is
    #    exactly why the arm-set lints could not see it and why three shipped
    #    comments asserted the omission was harmless.
    Mutex[Inner] mx = Mutex[Inner](Inner(seed()))
    Guard[Inner] gd = mx.lock()
    inc(&gd.fd)
    print(gd.fd)

